00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
by an older,

552
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
bigger scout, using some

553
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
really foul language.

554
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
when my wife and I found out what was said,

555
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
we were just about came unglued.

556
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
the next Monday night the scout masters

557
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
and the other adults met with

558
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
the older scout who had gone

559
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
after my son in this way

560
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
and they gave him a service project to report,

561
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
to write on the scout law,

562
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
and he had to make a public apology.

563
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
until this is done, he was not allowed

564
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
to participate with the troop.

565
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
my wife and I kind of feel like

566
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
this is a slap on the wrist.

567
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
we think this kind of verbal bullying

568
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
creates an unsafe environment.

569
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
we don't think anybody's filed

570
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
any kind of incident report with the council

571
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
and we have asked to meet with

572
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:17,000
everyone involved again.

573
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:19,000
if this meeting doesn't go well

574
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:21,000
and they don't do what we think should be done,

575
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:23,000
we're going to pull our son out of this troop.

576
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
my question is: are we

577
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
overreacting? well,

578
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
since you asked, yes,

579
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
you are definitely overreacting a little.

580
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,000
certainly, scouting should be a safe place

581
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
in every sense of the word,

582
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
but this doesn't mean we're going to be able to

583
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
make it an absolutely

584
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
safe place that prevents

585
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,000
every single bad thing from occurring.

586
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
that's impossible.

587
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
now, this is going to sound a little strange,

588
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
so listen carefully and hear me out.

589
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
I know how upsetting it can be.

590
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:53,000
I know that your son is 11

591
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:55,000
and there was a much bigger boy.

592
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:57,000
it's tough and difficult with him

593
00:22:57,000 --> 00:22:59,000
and I know that that can just be

594
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:01,000
not only terrifying and shocking.

595
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,000
it can really set

596
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:05,000
parents off.

597
00:23:05,000 --> 00:23:07,000
we not only can't prevent bad things like this

598
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,000
from occurring.

599
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:11,000
I think, when it does,

600
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:13,000
we should welcome the opportunity

601
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:15,000
that had happened in a scout troop

602
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:17,000
and we have the wherewithal

603
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:19,000
to resolve situations like this.

604
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:21,000
it's going to help the younger boy out

605
00:23:21,000 --> 00:23:23,000
and the older boy too.

606
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:25,000
I think it's probably more damaging to the future

607
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,000
of our children

608
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,000
to raise them in some bubble

609
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:31,000
of absolute safety

610
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:33,000
than to let them learn from occasional

611
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:35,000
difficulties. it's our hope that

612
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,000
scouting is always going to be a positive

613
00:23:37,000 --> 00:23:39,000
and safe place

614
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,000
and that scouts will always conduct themselves

615
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:43,000
in the highest level of behavior

616
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
and courtesy. now, when they don't,

617
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:47,000
it can be shocking

618
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:49,000
and unsettling, and I don't minimize that at all.

619
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:51,000
okay, it's very natural

620
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:53,000
to feel that way,

621
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:55,000
but you have to realize too

622
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:57,000
that it's an opportunity to reach out

623
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
and help all the scouts involved

624
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:01,000
in the situation that you describe.

625
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:03,000
I've had this sort of thing happen before

626
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:05,000
many times over in three decades

627
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:07,000
as a scout master,

628
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:09,000
and I don't really find it all that shocking anymore.

629
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:11,000
to tell you the truth,

630
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:13,000
it's a relatively rare occurrence,

631
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:15,000
but it's familiar enough.

632
00:24:15,000 --> 00:24:17,000
I would first sit down with the scout

633
00:24:17,000 --> 00:24:19,000
who was doing the bullying and the attacking

634
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:21,000
of what happened.

635
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:23,000
I'd ask to tell me how his actions

636
00:24:23,000 --> 00:24:25,000
either corresponded with the scout oath and law

637
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:27,000
or fell short of them.

638
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:29,000
we'd go on to discuss what would need to happen next.

639
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,000
I would probably guide him towards

640
00:24:31,000 --> 00:24:33,000
making an apology to the scout

641
00:24:33,000 --> 00:24:35,000
and a commitment to not

642
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:37,000
repeating that kind of behavior,

643
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:39,000
and once we had figured out exactly

644
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:41,000
how we'd be moving forward.

645
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:43,000
I would meet with that scout and his parents

646
00:24:43,000 --> 00:24:45,000
and we would all have a discussion.

647
00:24:45,000 --> 00:24:47,000
I would be leading the discussion,

648
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:49,000
but the scout is the one who needs to talk.

649
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:51,000
I would have him describe the situation

650
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:53,000
to his parents,

651
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:55,000
the discussion that we had had about it,

652
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:57,000
and then to tell his parents

653
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,000
what we decided needed to happen. next.

654
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:01,000
I would make sure that the parents

655
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:03,000
understood what went on

656
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:05,000
and I would tell them that they would have to assure me

657
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,000
that this sort of thing would never be repeated

658
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:09,000
and that, if it was,

659
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:11,000
it would probably require me to ask

660
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:13,000
their son to leave our troop.

661
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:15,000
and then the parents can take whatever actions

662
00:25:15,000 --> 00:25:17,000
they see fit,

663
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:19,000
so far as corrective punishment is involved,

664
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:21,000
with their son.

665
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:23,000
I would never, ever

666
00:25:23,000 --> 00:25:25,000
impose corrective punishment

667
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:27,000
like writing apology letters

668
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:29,000
or doing service projects or reports

669
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:31,000
or whatever,

670
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:33,000
and I don't suggest that this is a good idea

671
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:35,000
for anyone else.

672
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:37,000
as a matter of fact, I think taking actions like that

673
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:39,000
is a very serious mistake.

674
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:41,000
Scouters don't use corrective punishment.

675
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,000
we do not punish,

676
00:25:43,000 --> 00:25:45,000
we do not discipline.

677
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:47,000
that's a parent's job.

678
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:49,000
what we do is we deal in proportionate

679
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:51,000
consequences.

680
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:53,000
we are partners with parents in raising their children

681
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:55,000
and we let parents know what's going on

682
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:57,000
and it's their option to employ

683
00:25:57,000 --> 00:25:59,000
corrective discipline or punishment,

684
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:01,000
not ours.

685
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:03,000
let's say, you know, if a scout

686
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:05,000
misbehaves in a way

687
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:07,000
that causes some kind of mess,

688
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:09,000
well the proportionate consequences of that

689
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:11,000
is he has to clean up the mess.

690
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:13,000
but as far as I'm concerned,

691
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:15,000
his parent is going to know

692
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:17,000
that he caused a mess and he had to clean it up

693
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:19,000
and I will tell the parents:

694
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:21,000
look, we don't have any kind of room for this kind of behavior.

695
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:23,000
so you need to correct it

696
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,000
and send him back to us so he can participate

697
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:27,000
in the troop. and if he repeats this kind of behavior,

698
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:29,000
we're going to have problems

699
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:31,000
and it's going to put his continued participation

700
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:33,000
with our troop in jeopardy.

701
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:35,000
so there's a huge difference between those

702
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:37,000
proportionate consequences and corrective punishment.

703
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:39,000
Scouters don't

704
00:26:39,000 --> 00:26:41,000
need to corrective punishment or discipline,

705
00:26:41,000 --> 00:26:43,000
just use the proportionate

706
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:45,000
consequences of the action.

707
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:47,000
then, in a partnership with parents,

708
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:49,000
you let them deal with the

709
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:51,000
any kind of corrective punishment

710
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:53,000
or discipline that needs to happen with that behavior.

711
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:55,000
listen, I hope that helps.

712
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:57,000
if you have a question

713
00:26:57,000 --> 00:26:59,000
or a problem and you want to get in touch with me,

714
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:01,000
you're going to find out how to do that

715
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:03,000
just after this scout master's minute

716
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:11,000
for a scout master's minute,

717
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:23,000
Norman was walking on a

718
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:25,000
beach in Florida

719
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:27,000
and he was thinking about

720
00:27:27,000 --> 00:27:29,000
a problem

721
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:31,000
and he bent down and he drew

722
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:33,000
a few characters in the sand

723
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:35,000
and he came up with his solution.

724
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:37,000
now Norman was born in

725
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:39,000
Atlantic City, New Jersey,

726
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:41,000
on September 6, 1921

727
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:43,000
and he graduated from Atlantic City High School

728
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:45,000
and he became an engineering student

729
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:47,000
at Drexel University.

730
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:49,000
right up the road from me in Philadelphia

731
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:51,000
it was the Drexel Institute of Technology.

732
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:53,000
when he was there in 1947

733
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:55,000
a fellow grad student,

734
00:27:55,000 --> 00:27:57,000
Bernard Silver,

735
00:27:57,000 --> 00:27:59,000
overheard a supermarket executive

736
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:01,000
asking the dean of students to figure out

737
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:03,000
how to capture product information

738
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:05,000
automatically at a checkout-

739
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:07,000
I think a whole lot of the requests- and he turned it down.

740
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:09,000
but Silver went on to mention

741
00:28:09,000 --> 00:28:11,000
that problem to Norman

742
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:13,000
and after talking about it for a while

743
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:15,000
and kind of working on some preliminary ideas,

744
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:17,000
they were both persuaded that they could create

745
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:19,000
the kind of technology

746
00:28:19,000 --> 00:28:21,000
needed to capture product information

747
00:28:21,000 --> 00:28:23,000
automatically at a supermarket checkout.

748
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:25,000
while they were convinced that they could

749
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:27,000
create this technology, they didn't really have

750
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:29,000
the details worked out.

751
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:31,000
and this takes us to the beach in Florida

752
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:33,000
and Norman making some characters

753
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:35,000
in the sand, because Norman

754
00:28:35,000 --> 00:28:37,000
had been a Boy Scout

755
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:39,000
and back in the 1930s and 40s

756
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:41,000
when you were a Boy Scout you had to learn

757
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:43,000
something called Morse Code

758
00:28:43,000 --> 00:28:45,000
and most of you would be familiar with. Morse Code

759
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:47,000
is a series of dots and dashes.

760
00:28:47,000 --> 00:28:49,000
Norman was drawing

761
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:51,000
dots and dashes in the sand,

762
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:53,000
so he stuck his finger

763
00:28:53,000 --> 00:28:55,000
in the sand and made a dot

764
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:57,000
and then he dragged that dot

765
00:28:57,000 --> 00:28:59,000
into a line.

766
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:01,000
then he made a dash

767
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:03,000
and made that into a line

768
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:05,000
and these lines

769
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:07,000
that were really just long dots and dashes

770
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:09,000
helped him crack the code

771
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:11,000
on coming up with a two-dimensional

772
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:13,000
linear Morse Code.

773
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:15,000
and he went back and he shared it

774
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:17,000
with his friend, Bernard Silver

775
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:19,000
and they used a little movie soundtrack technology

776
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:21,000
and they applied for a patent

777
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:23,000
in 1949 and they got it.

778
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:25,000
so long story short,

779
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:27,000
after two decades

780
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:29,000
Norman and Bernard's patent

781
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:31,000
had been sold to the International Business

782
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:33,000
Machines Company

783
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:35,000
where IBM.

784
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:37,000
Norman was an employee of IBM at the time

785
00:29:37,000 --> 00:29:39,000
and he helped them develop

786
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:41,000
the first scanning technology

787
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:43,000
that everybody uses now

788
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:45,000
whenever we go to a supermarket checkout

789
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:47,000
or convenience store checkout anywhere.

790
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:49,000
in 1971

791
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:51,000
the Universal Product Code

792
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:53,000
came into being and by 1974

793
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:55,000
the first item

794
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:57,000
was scanned in

795
00:29:57,000 --> 00:29:59,000
an Ohio supermarket.

796
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:01,000
it happened to be a pack of chewing gum.

797
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:03,000
so anytime we go into a store right now,

798
00:30:03,000 --> 00:30:05,000
or any product you have to hand,

799
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:07,000
has a Universal Product Code on it.

800
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:09,000
if you look at it, it's a set of lines,

801
00:30:09,000 --> 00:30:11,000
and those lines

802
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:13,000
were created by former Boy Scout

803
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:15,000
Norman Woodward

804
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:17,000
based on the fact that he learned

805
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:19,000
Morse Code

806
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:21,000
when he was in Boy Scouts.

807
00:30:21,000 --> 00:30:23,000
so pay attention, Scouts, because you never know

808
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:25,000
what you're going to pick up

809
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:27,000
and it's a very important part of your life:

810
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:29,000
Music,

811
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:31,000
Music,

812
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:33,000
Music,

813
00:30:33,000 --> 00:30:35,000
Music,

814
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:37,000
Music.

